  "narrative": "The 2026 Kansas City Royals opened their season with flashes of the electricity that had made them dangerous in recent years, but those moments of brilliance were painfully brief. Salvador Perez announced his presence with a three-run blast in mid-April, and Lane Thomas delivered a stunning three-run homer on April 26th that briefly made Kauffman Stadium feel like a place where magic could happen. Bobby Witt Jr. continued to be the heartbeat of the lineup, collecting timely hits throughout the year, and young catcher Carter Jensen showed genuine promise. But for every highlight, the bullpen — particularly Lucas Erceg — had a way of unraveling what the offense carefully built.\n\nThe middle months told a grim story. Lucas Erceg became almost synonymous with late-game disaster, surrendering lead after lead in April and May, and the relievers who followed him were no safer. Alex Lange was repeatedly victimized in July, giving up home runs to Ty France, Willy Adames, and then — most devastatingly — surrendering a Royce Lewis triple that shredded a close game on July 28th. The bullpen's collective failures cast a long shadow over a team that was never able to string together sustained winning.\n\nJuly and early August brought the most gut-wrenching blow of the season: Matt Strahm watched Kody Clemens crush a grand slam on July 30th, a nearly -0.93 WPA swing that was the single most damaging moment of the entire year. That play encapsulated everything wrong with the 2026 Royals — a winnable game turned into a rout in one catastrophic moment. John Schreiber, Steven Cruz, and Nate Pearson all added their own painful chapters to the bullpen saga as the losses piled up.\n\nBy September, the Royals were playing out the string at 69-93, but at least their young players gave the fanbase something to look forward to. Carter Jensen slugged his 27th home run on September 25th, a sign that the organization's catching pipeline was finally bearing fruit. John Rave contributed a two-run shot, and Vinnie Pasquantino showed some life late in the year. It was a season defined by squandered opportunities and a bullpen that never found its footing — a 24-game negative run differential ultimately telling the truest story of a team still searching for the right formula.",
